<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Aghlabid emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 909 CE · capital Kairouan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Aghlabid emirs of Ifriqiya, who ruled Tunisia for the Abbasids, built Kairouan's Great Mosque and conquered Sicily.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 800 the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim ibn al-Aghlab</a>, an Arab army commander, the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> as a hereditary office in return for annual tribute. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> and their palace city al-Abbasiyya, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a>, as autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Relying on an Arab military elite and slave soldiers, they faced frequent army revolts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-i">Ziyadat Allah I</a> launched the conquest of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/theme-of-sicily">Byzantine Sicily</a> in 827, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabid</a> fleets raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 870. They rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a>, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">ribat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a> and great water cisterns, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law under the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahnun">Sahnun</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 890s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a>, mobilised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</a>, rose against them. In 909 the last emir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-iii">Ziyadat Allah III</a>, fled to the east, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aghlabid-emirate.jpg" alt="Aghlabid emirate" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Nanoxyde at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aghlabids_Dynasty_800_-_909_(AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 909 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kairouan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibrahim ibn al-Aghlab, Ziyadat Allah I, Ibrahim II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>, <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aghlabid Emirate, Aghlabids, Emirate of Ifriqiya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=854&amp;focus=aghlabid-emirate" aria-label="Aghlabid emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4935 889 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aghlabid emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5255 1145L5811 1337M5255 1145L6134 1224